\begin{proposition}
Let $H=\langle g_0,\ldots,g_{k-1}\rangle\leq G$. Suppose that
$A\in M_{r,n}(F)$ and $R\in M_{n,r}(F)$, where $0<r<n$, satisfy
\[
 AR=I_r,\qquad Ag_j=(Ag_jR)A\quad(0\leq j<k).
\]
If $z\in G$ and a row $a_i$ of $A$ satisfy
\[
 a_i z\ne (a_i zR)A,
\]
then $H\ne G$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The identity $AR=I_r$ proves that the rows of $A$ are independent. Set
$W=\operatorname{rowspan}(A)$. A row $u$ belongs to $W$ if and only if
$u=(uR)A$. Indeed, $u=cA$ implies $(uR)A=cARA=cA$, and the converse
follows from the displayed expression. Every $g_j$ therefore maps $W$
into itself. Since it is invertible, it preserves $W$. Thus $H$ preserves
$W$, whereas the stipulated member $z$ of $G$ does not. Hence $z\notin H$.
\end{proof}

